# L5R 5E - SHADOWLANDS: THE NEZUMI (LORE)

**Version:** 0.4
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game, 5th Edition - Shadowlands (Chapter 1)
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## THE NEZUMI

The nezumi are ratlike humanoids whose civilization existed long before the fall of Fu Leng. The Dark Kami's violent descent destroyed the civilization their ancestors had built, and the creation of the Shadowlands turned their homeland hostile. Unable to flee into human lands, the survivors created a new society of nomadic groups that move from place to place to survive.

While many Rokugani consider the nezumi to be mythological monsters, Crab samurai know better. Hiruma scouts foster communication with nezumi to acquire knowledge of dangerous areas of the Shadowlands, and the Kaiu family relies on the Third Whisker tribe that lives under the Wall to aid in its defense. Crab peasants leave offerings of food, believing nezumi help keep malignant forces at bay.

---

### Physiology

Furred, with clawed feet and hands, ratlike noses, and long tails, even nezumi who walk upright and wear scavenged samurai armor cannot pass for human. They average roughly four feet in height, though this varies wildly between tribes, as does fur color and style of dress.

Nezumi live three or four years on average. Their impressive rate of reproduction sustains their numbers. Rememberers, Dreamers, and tribal leaders often live a decade or more, ensuring tribes maintain their identities and histories consistently over many generations.

---

### Yesterday — The Golden Age

The nezumi Rememberers tell of a golden age when their city was a center of learning and culture ruled by a monarch and council. Their army kept other races at bay, their philosophers filled a great library with wisdom, and their culture was considered superior to all others.

Then a falling mountain crashed through their city, sending it to Yume-do, the Realm of Dreams. Evil leaked into the world, and corruption spread across their lands. All beings were transformed into vile creatures — all except the nezumi, who alone proved incorruptible.

---

### Untainted

Whether their fortitude is truly a matter of moral fiber or a quirk of physiology is debated by both nezumi and Crab scholars. The Kuni family has attempted to solve this mystery through terrible experiments. Before the nezumi had allies among the Rokugani, they were considered vermin, and nezumi caught scavenging were passed to the Kuni for investigation. The Kuni have never shared details of their abhorrent investigations.

Now that the Crab have an uneasy alliance with the nezumi, the Kuni lack a steady supply of subjects. Stories of Kuni wardens seizing live subjects for study arise from time to time, and some are certainly true.

---

### Nezumi Magic — The Dreamers

Dreamers are the closest thing nezumi have to shugenja, but they function differently. Dreamers are attuned to Yume-do, the Realm of Dreams, communing with Transcendents (nezumi ancestors who dwell there) and dream spirits known as baku.

**Dreamwalking** allows Dreamers to communicate with each other and with residents of Yume-do. They can visit the dreams of other creatures and people to learn, communicate, inflict nightmares, or assert limited control.

**Dreamshaping** allows Dreamers to divine and change the identities of things within Yume-do. The spiritual ramifications can be permanent — while Dreamers cannot change physical form, they can alter a destiny. This is usually used to aid allies, though selfish Dreamers may steal destinies for themselves.

**Dreamseeking** allows Dreamers to enter Yume-do bodily, find someone immersed in a dream, and return to the Mortal Realm through their dream, effectively tunneling through realms. This ability is taxing but has allowed Dreamers to escape otherwise-certain death on many occasions.

---

## NEZUMI TRIBES

The nezumi identify themselves by their tribes, which are usually their extended family. Loyalty is paramount, and betrayal is the one crime most tribes punish by death.

---

### The Third Whisker

A small but important tribe that largely makes its home underneath the Kaiu Wall. Small in number and physical size, its members are quick to flee and hide. The Kaiu tolerate their presence, as when the Wall comes under attack, the tribe protects its home with admirable ferocity.

The leader of the Third Whisker is also its Dreamer, and the tribe boasts two Rememberers with more in training. Unlike some nezumi groups who look primarily to the future, this group considers the continuation of its ancestors' history and arts equally important as survival.

---

### The Cracked Bone

Stronger and larger than most nezumi, some reaching five feet in height. They carry weapons retrieved from fallen bushi or fashioned from bone, stone, or even obsidian. They pierce their ears, noses, and tails with carved decorations and wear scavenged armor.

The Cracked Bone actively seek battle against Shadowlands creatures to prove themselves worthy. Their young must bloody their claws to achieve adulthood. Their leader holds position by virtue of martial skill, and when they grow too old to defeat all challengers, a victorious warrior kills them and takes their place.

---

### The Burnt Paws

According to their Rememberers, the Burnt Paws descend from the bellicose Ghost Paws, who preferred stealing from the Crab rather than scavenging Shadowlands filth. After a disastrous raid on a town of veteran bushi, the Ghost Paws were driven into a burning building. Only those who fled over burning wood survived.

The Burnt Paws are cowardly by their own admission, avoiding conflicts of all kinds. They eat Shadowlands weeds and insects, sleep in deep burrows, and wear discarded scraps. Yet their Rememberer knows where the Ghost Paws' weapons lie hidden, for the time when the tribe must become warriors again.

---

### The Tattered Ear

Tales are told of a tribe that managed to flee through human lands and now lives hidden within the Shinomen Forest. The nezumi consider the Shinomen a utopia — a home without Shadowlands creatures or human intervention. Other nezumi whisper about the progress the Tattered Ear must be making.

---

## NEZUMI LIFE

Many villages consist of temporary dwellings and permanent burrows. Villages do not stay in one place for long; nezumi pack up when food grows scarce, at the first sign of danger, or due to a Dreamer's warning. Most tribes regularly return to the same locations where they already burrowed escape tunnels and know the terrain.

All tribes have a leader — in some the greatest warrior, in others a gifted Dreamer or wizened Rememberer. When a tribe becomes too small, members find another depleted tribe to create a new one. When too large, it splits. Loyalty within the tribe is absolute.

### Dangerous Nezumi

Most nezumi keep their distance from humans, but some isolated, warlike tribes consider humans the enemy. These hardened survivors live in the most dangerous parts of the Shadowlands. More commonly encountered are uncivilized nezumi who grew up without tribal guidance, and outcast nezumi banished for misdeeds — either type might pose a threat to lone scouts or wounded samurai.
